# idle: back off polling by idle entries, not time. 0039's backoff stopped polling for 10 ms, doubling
# to 250 ms, after an epoch of polls that did not pay; a CPU woken a few dozen times a second (Frigate:
# a camera's frames and its Python threads' timers, about 37 idle entries a second per vCPU on 30)
# came out of the 10 ms before its next idle entry, so polling went on as if there were no backoff:
# 11,703 backoffs started in three and a half minutes and polling unchanged. The backoff is now that
# many idle entries skipped, poll_epoch (16) at first, doubling to idle.poll_backoff_max (1024) while
# polling still does not pay; a run of short sleeps still ends it early, and a model talking still
# polls through it. idle.backoffs reports, per CPU, backoffs started, ended early by short sleeps, and
# polls let through by accelerator traffic.
